fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1109", description: some(value: "SPLIT THE ORDERING TOWER THREE WAYS — `PartialOrd` / `WeakOrd` / `Ord` — so that \"total\" and \"antisymmetric\" stop being one name. DECIDED (user, 2026-08-15) during WI-456 review; the design and every number below were MEASURED in that conversation, not predicted.\n\nTHE DEFECT. `Ord` today claims BOTH totality (every pair comparable) AND that its kernel is `Eq` — the latter written as a law, `compare_eq: eq(?a, ?b) <=> eq(compare(?a, ?b), 0)` (ordered.anthill). That biconditional is FALSE for a witness this repo ships and drives. MEASURED: `ByLength` (wi844_sorted_set_driver_test) provides `Ord[T = String]` with `compare(\"zz\",\"aa\") = 0` while `String.eq(\"zz\",\"aa\") = false`. It loads only because the law is DORMANT — eq.anthill's header says these obligations are \"not discharged or refuted per instance\". `ByFst`/`BySnd` (wi858_pair_orderings_test) are the same shape, ordering a `Pair` by one component.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE IS OBSERVABLE, NOT THEORETICAL, and the stdlib already relies on it. `SortedSet.insertSorted`'s else arm treats `compare = 0` as identity (\"already present: a SET, so no duplicate\"), so a `SortedSet` under a coarse comparator stores EQUIVALENCE CLASSES with a chosen representative. MEASURED:\n * {\"zz\",\"aa\",\"b\"} under ByLength -> 2 elements; the same three under the host `Ord[String]` -> 3. (Control: the collapse is the comparator's.)\n * `union` is LEFT-BIASED and therefore NOT COMMUTATIVE. a = {\"zz\"}, b = {\"aa\"} (one ByLength class): `union(a,b)` = [\"zz\"], `union(b,a)` = [\"aa\"], both size 1. `merge`'s equal-heads arm (sortedset.anthill:163) keeps `x`, the left operand's element. Control under the host order: both strings survive, size 2, so operand order cannot matter there.\n * Therefore sortedset.anthill's EQUALITY paragraph over-reaches as written — \"the sorted item list is canonical, so two sets with the same members are the same term\" is true only where the kernel coincides with `Eq`.\n\nTHE DESIGN. Two independent questions get two names, with `requires` doing the composition:\n\n  PartialOrd[T]  requires PartialEq[T]          -- comparison; INCOMPARABLE pairs possible (Float/NaN)\n  WeakOrd[T]     requires Eq[T], PartialOrd[T]  -- `compare`; TOTAL; CONGRUENT w.r.t. Eq:\n                                                --   eq(x, y) -> compare(x, y) = 0\n  Ord[T]         requires WeakOrd[T]            -- adds ONLY the converse:\n                                                --   compare(x, y) = 0 -> eq(x, y)\n\n`WeakOrd` carries `compare` and the reflexivity/antisymmetry/orientation laws; `Ord` adds NO operation, only the law — deliberately mirroring proposal 004's own shape, where \"`Eq` adds *only* the reflexivity law (a marker + obligation, no new operation)\" over `PartialEq`.\n\nWHY THE CONGRUENCE LAW IS THE ONE `WeakOrd` OWNS. `eq(x,y) -> compare(x,y) = 0` says `compare` is WELL-DEFINED ON `Eq`-CLASSES: the kernel of `compare` is a union of `Eq`-classes, never a splitting of one. That is exactly what keeps a sorted structure well-formed (no `Eq`-class lands in two positions), and it is the weakest law that does. `ByLength` satisfies it. `Ord`'s extra law is the converse, which `ByLength` does not satisfy and `Int64`/`String` do.\n\nWHY `requires Eq` SURVIVES, AND IS NOW JUSTIFIED RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL. It sits on `WeakOrd` as the RELATUM the congruence law is stated against — the order does not SUPPLY the equality, it names the equality it must respect. This also answers WI-456's second clause (`maybe local Eq should be derived from Ord?`) without touching coherence: a PROOF IS NOT A SUPPLIER. A law adds no `sort_ops` entry, no provision row, no dispatch candidate, so relating `Ord` to `Eq` never creates the second `eq` supplier `AmbiguousEqDispatch` (WI-837) exists to refuse. Deriving one WOULD, which is why `Eq` still may not be derived: equality dispatches from UNIFICATION (`sem_eq_core`, kb/resolve.rs), where `build_eq_dispatch_index` records there is \"no later site to complain from\".\n\nNAMING, and why NOT `TotalOrd`. Totality is what `WeakOrd` already adds over `PartialOrd`; the top spec adds ANTISYMMETRY. Calling it `TotalOrd` would attach the name to the wrong property — this repo's recurring one-name-two-questions defect. `PreOrd` was considered and dropped: a preorder need not be total, and this one is. \"Weak order\" is the standard term for a total preorder, and C++20's `<compare>` splits these THREE with these exact examples — `partial_ordering` (NaN), `weak_ordering` (equivalence WITHOUT substitutability, canonically case-insensitive compare; by-length is the same shape), `strong_ordering` (equivalent implies substitutable). One-to-one with `PartialOrd`/`WeakOrd`/`Ord`. That is not only aesthetic: a carrier's ordering strength is exactly what decides the comparison category its C++ `operator<=>` returns, so this taxonomy feeds WI-1107 (proposal 004 step 4's unshipped total half — a generated entity struct carries no `operator==`/`<=>` at all) instead of having to be invented there.\n\nPRIOR ART, for the record: Haskell `class Eq a => Ord a` documents a total order INCLUDING antisymmetry and `compare x y == EQ` iff `x == y`, unchecked — and `Data.Map.union` is documented LEFT-BIASED, the same non-commutativity measured above, acknowledged in container docs rather than ruled out by the class. Scala `Ordering[A]` is total in COMPARABILITY only; `Ordering.by(_.length)` is legal and `TreeSet` collapses under it. Lean 4's `Ord` is bare `compare` with NO superclass and NO laws, with lawfulness factored into separate classes (`OrientedOrd`/`TransOrd`, and `LawfulEqOrd`, whose statement `compare a b = .eq <-> a = b` is precisely this ticket's `Ord` law). Proposal 004 already chose Lean's model for the equality side (\"the checked-law decision is Lean's model, not Rust's\"), so factoring the ordering side the same way is consistency, not novelty.\n\nMIGRATION — MEASURED, AND IT IS ADDITIVE. Each floor is written EXPLICITLY today: `anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill` writes all four of `PartialEq`/`Eq`/`PartialOrd`/`Ord`, and `string.anthill` likewise. So 058 §3.8's floor-DERIVATION clause is still only proposed, and this ticket does not depend on it — a carrier adds one `provides WeakOrd[…]` line beside the `provides Ord[…]` it already has, exactly as it already adds `provides PartialOrd` beside it.\n * STRONG carriers, unchanged in meaning, one line added: `Int64`, `String`, `BigInt` (anthill-stl bindings). `TotalFloat` is untouched — it provides `Eq` and deliberately NOT `Ord`.\n * `Pair` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill) needs TWO conditional provisions, not one: lexicographic `Pair` is strong iff both components are, weak iff both are weak — `provides Ord[Pair] :- Ord[A], Ord[B]` beside `provides WeakOrd[Pair] :- WeakOrd[A], WeakOrd[B]`. Per-provision conditions are delivered (WI-869), so this needs no new machinery — and it is the `PartialEq`/`Eq` pair on the same sort, one relation over.\n * `SortedSet` slot becomes `requires O: WeakOrd[T]` — ONE word. Strong carriers still satisfy it through the chain, so `SortedSet[T = Int64, O = Int64]` is unaffected.\n * Reclassify to `provides WeakOrd`: `ByLength` (wi844), `ByFst`/`BySnd` (wi858). `Alphabetical` (wi844) is strong and stays `Ord`.\n * `PartialOrd`'s four default bodies (`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`) carry an op-scoped `requires Ord[T]` and call `Ord.compare`; both become `WeakOrd`, since the derivation needs only the weak order. NOTE the standing interaction recorded at ordered.anthill cost (2) and in WI-879: that op-scoped requirement's CALL-SITE half is currently MASKED by `check_one_spec_op_requirement`'s early return for a builtin functor. This ticket must not silently change which spec that mask hides.\n * Fixtures writing `fact Ord[T = …]` that then flow into a `SortedSet` slot need a `WeakOrd` row too — candidates seen: wi844, wi857, wi1094, wi1102. Fixtures that merely provide and require `Ord` are unaffected.\n\nALSO IN SCOPE, because they are claims that become false or unscoped: sortedset.anthill's EQUALITY paragraph (scope the canonicity claim to a kernel coinciding with `Eq`, and STATE the quotient semantics and the left-biased `union` as contract rather than leaving them to be discovered); ordered.anthill's WI-456 note added 2026-08-15 (it currently explains why `Eq` is not derived FROM `Ord` — still true, but it must move to the right floor); the kernel-language.md paragraph that ends \"the total `compare`/laws live on `Ord`, which `requires Eq, PartialOrd`\".\n\nDOC OWNERSHIP: a new LIBRARY PROPOSAL (007 is free) continuing 004 the way 004 continues 051 — 051 split structural from semantic equality, 004 split partial from total equality, this splits WEAK from STRONG (antisymmetric) ordering. 004 keeps its own text; the new proposal states the rule and the spec absorbs it.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE. Making any of these laws LIVE. Nothing discharges `eq_refl` or `compare_eq` per instance today, so `Ord`-vs-`WeakOrd` is enforced by which spec a carrier DECLARES, not by a proof. The law-discharge capability is what would catch a `ByLength` declared under the wrong spec, and it is a separate arc.\n\nACCEPTANCE. A carrier providing only `WeakOrd` sorts correctly and is REFUSED where `Ord` is required — driven, with the value asserted, not merely loaded. `SortedSet` under a `WeakOrd`-only comparator still collapses classes and its `union` is still left-biased, now as declared contract with a test pinning both directions ({\"zz\"} u {\"aa\"} vs {\"aa\"} u {\"zz\"}). The strong stdlib carriers keep working unchanged through the chain. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-15T21:35:14Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "DESIGN CORRECTED BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION (user, 2026-08-15): the floor links are `provides`, NOT `requires`. `Ord provides WeakOrd[T = T]`, `WeakOrd provides PartialOrd[T = T]`, `Eq provides PartialEq[T = T]`. The ticket text above says `requires` throughout; read it as `provides` for the three floor links (the RELATUM clauses stay `requires`: `WeakOrd requires Eq[T]`, `PartialOrd requires PartialEq[T]`, `NonEq requires PartialEq[T]`).\n\nWHY IT IS BETTER, in 058 §3.8's own words: 'Deriving beats writing both floors: two hand-written provisions permit a bundle whose halves disagree, whereas one compare cannot.' Today the bindings write all four floors by hand, and they CAN disagree — EqDispatchIndex sweeps BOTH `PartialEq` and `Eq` precisely because a carrier may bind a different `eq` under each, and two distinct impls is AmbiguousEqDispatch. Deriving makes that unrepresentable (the repo's make-illegal-states-unrepresentable rule) and turns the migration from ADD A LINE PER CARRIER into DELETE THE REDUNDANT FLOORS.\n\nIT IS SPELLABLE TODAY — no new machinery. A SPEC providing a SPEC is delivered: `stream.anthill:108` has `provides Iterable[C = Stream, Element = T, E = E]` inside `sort Stream`, and WI-495/WI-496 DELETED List's explicit `provides Iterable` + `iterator` because `List provides Stream` + `Stream provides Iterable` compose through `transitive_provision_view`. That is exactly this shape.\n\nIT ALSO CLOSES ordered.anthill COST (1), unasked. That note records that PartialOrd's four default bodies read `Ord.compare`, which PartialOrd does not require, so 'a carrier providing PartialOrd ALONE inherits a body that cannot run for it — the load check sees a runnable default and says backed', and names a per-provision condition (058 §3.8) as the proper fix. With `WeakOrd provides PartialOrd`, the provision row for the derived floor COMES FROM WeakOrd, so the derivation is backed exactly where a `compare` exists. Float keeps its explicit `provides PartialOrd` (fill silence, never overwrite speech) and maps all four in its binding.\n\nONE DEPARTURE FROM §3.8 TO RECORD RATHER THAN HIDE. §3.8 states the predicate 'derive the provision for a required floor iff the upper floor's laws determine that floor's surface AND the floor is selectable — PartialOrd qualifies; Eq does not.' `Eq provides PartialEq` fails that second conjunct as literally written, since the Eq family is never selectable (§3.7). It is admitted here because the conjunct guards against creating an unresolvable RIVAL, and within one tower where the upper spec adds NO OPERATION there is nothing to rival: `Eq.eq` IS the inherited `PartialEq.eq`, so the derived row supplies the same impl. The derivation must FILL SILENCE, NEVER OVERWRITE SPEECH — a carrier already writing `provides PartialEq` keeps its own row, the discipline kernel-language.md already records for the composite Eq derivation ('the derivation skips a carrier that already provides'). If that turns out not to hold at load, the fallback is to keep `Eq requires PartialEq` and apply `provides` only to the ordering tower.", at: "2026-08-15T16:01:58Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "IN PROGRESS 2026-08-15, increments 1 and 2 landed, suite 2792 pass / 47 fail (from 68 at the start of the migration). NOT delivered.\n\nTHE ARCHITECTURE CHANGED MID-FLIGHT, and the wrong one is worth recording. First attempt taught the READERS of the provides relation about forwarding: the WI-343 provider-requirements check, then witness selection. Patching two surfaced a THIRD (the resolver's step-0 pin filter), which is the standing many-producers lesson. 058 §3.8 already said the right thing — the missing piece is 'the provision ROW a requires PartialOrd[X] goal finds', one clause over the relation — so the pass now MATERIALIZES the row (derive_forwarded_provisions, kb/typing.rs) and every reader consumes it unchanged, the same reason eq_derive asserts rows rather than teaching sort_provides about composites.\n\nINCREMENT 1 (still needed, and independent): the WI-343 check refused `Ord provides WeakOrd` outright — 'Ord does not provide Eq'. Right for a carrier, wrong for a spec-to-spec provision where the requirement belongs to the eventual carrier. `forwarded_to_requires` discharges it through the PROVIDER's own requires, which is where the obligation is written and how it reaches the carrier. Narrow by construction: fires only when EVERY binding is the provider's own abstract param, so ByLength provides Ord[T = String], Pair provides Ord[Pair] :- …, and the WI-644 self-carrier route are untouched. `Stream provides Iterable` never hit this because Iterable declares no requires at all.\n\nINCREMENT 2, and PLACEMENT WAS THE WHOLE THING. Derived rows were correct from the first run (traced: ByLength/Alphabetical/Int64/String/BigInt all gained WeakOrd rows) and every call site still refused. Placed beside eq_derive::run — below req_insertion — the rows existed and were read by nobody. Moved beside derive_total_eq, which carries a comment stating exactly this ('a provision reaches a call site only through the typer's CallClass tagging and req_insertion's rewrite, so a derived Eq asserted after them exists but is read by nobody'), and wi844 went 5/13 -> 16/2 -> 18/18. THE NEIGHBOURING COMMENT HAD ALREADY MEASURED MY BUG.\n\nWHAT IS PROVEN: wi844_sorted_set_driver_test 18/18 with carriers writing ONE `provides Ord` and no WeakOrd row — the point of the forwarding. sortedset requires O: WeakOrd[T]; the two-ordering driver, the type error across orderings, and the eval value assertions all hold.\n\nDELIBERATE UNDER-DERIVATION, stated at the site: a CONDITIONAL provision derives nothing (the !kb.is_fact guard). Copying only the head would drop the ':- goals' tail and claim the floor unconditionally — the exact over-claim WI-1033's ProvisionConditionsTooWeak refuses, manufactured by the deriver. So a conditional provider writes both floors by hand; pair.anthill does, and its two tails DIFFER (weak iff both components weak, strong iff both strong), so one copied tail could not have served both anyway. Deriving a conditional row = asserting a rule with the source's body = the next increment.\n\nREMAINING 47, three mechanical classes: (a) fixtures calling the now-qualified WeakOrd.compare without importing WeakOrd; (b) assertion strings naming anthill.prelude.Ord where the message now says WeakOrd; (c) conditional providers (wi858 ByFst/BySnd, wi869, wi870) that must write `provides WeakOrd[…] :- …` by hand for the reason above.\n\nTO CHECK BEFORE COMMIT: the two SELECTION-reader patches (check_witness_provides_spec's transitive fallback, witness_answers_through_forwarder) may now be redundant given the materialized rows — they were written under the abandoned architecture. Try removing them and re-run; keep only what a back-out proves is load-bearing. Also NOT started: eq.anthill's `Eq provides PartialEq` half, kernel-language.md, and library proposal 007.", at: "2026-08-15T16:57:40Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "MIGRATION COMPLETE, FULL WORKSPACE GREEN 2026-08-15 (rustland/scripts/test.sh — every binary ok, wi_tests 2839 passed / 0 failed, 0 compile errors). Not yet delivered: three scope items below remain.\n\nFINAL SHAPE. PartialOrd (may be incomparable) / WeakOrd (total + congruent, compare lives here) / Ord (adds only the antisymmetry law). `Ord provides WeakOrd[T = T]` AND `Ord requires Eq[T], PartialOrd[T], WeakOrd[T]`. Both clauses are load-bearing and they are NOT redundant — measured: `provides` says every Ord CARRIER is a WeakOrd carrier (what the derivation reads), `requires` says every Ord DICTIONARY contains a WeakOrd one (what a USE site reads). With provides alone, a body written `requires Ord[T] … WeakOrd.compare(a, b)` fails to resolve its own call — i.e. every Ord-constrained generic in every downstream program. That one clause took the suite 31 -> 13.\n\nKERNEL, two pieces, +264/-4 across typing.rs and load.rs, most of it comment:\n (1) `forwarded_to_requires` — WI-343's provider-requirements check now discharges a spec-to-spec forwarding through the PROVIDER's own requires, relocating the obligation to the eventual carrier. Narrow: fires only when every binding is the provider's own abstract param.\n (2) `derive_forwarded_provisions` — materializes the forwarded provision ROW, 058 §3.8's clause. PLACEMENT WAS DECISIVE: beside eq_derive::run (below req_insertion) the rows were correct and read by nobody; moved beside derive_total_eq, whose own comment states exactly that constraint, wi844 went 5/13 -> 18/18.\n\nTWO REAL BUGS FOUND IN MY OWN PASS, both by the suite:\n * A CONDITIONAL provision was being derived UNCONDITIONALLY. The `is_fact` guard does NOT catch these — a `:- goals` tail rides in separate ProvidesConditionInfo facts (WI-869), so the row is a plain fact. Measured: `Cell provides Ord[…] :- Ord[E]` yielded an unconditional `Cell provides WeakOrd` that failed its own requires and built a cyclic construction. Now guarded on provision_conditions; the pass UNDER-derives for conditional providers, which write both floors by hand.\n * The two SELECTION-reader patches written under the abandoned architecture were REDUNDANT once rows are materialized. Backed out and measured: 2792/47 identical before and after, binary mtime confirmed rebuilt (14s after the source edit), so this is a real back-out and not a skipped build.\n\nFIXTURE MIGRATION, 68 -> 0, and most of it was NOT cosmetic. Beyond imports and message strings, three classes were semantic and are recorded at their sites: (a) a chosen rival comparator belongs on the WEAK floor — wi1094's `Descending` declaring `fact Ord[Int64]` put a second provider on the floor Int64 itself occupies, making a bracket-less `Ord[Int64]` ambiguous; (b) `LexFst`'s element slots are WeakOrd, because a lexicographic order reads only `compare` and claims nothing about component kernels, so Ord slots demanded more than the body used; (c) `gt` witnesses WeakOrd, not Ord, so `requires(Ord[T])` can no longer be grounded by a gt() call — correct, since Ord declares no operation of its own that any body could witness.\n\nALSO MEASURED, wider blast radius than the ordering tower and truthful: the pass derives `Relation provides Stream` (Relation provides LogicalStream[T = T, E = E], LogicalStream provides Stream[T = T, E = E], both identity forwardings). sort_provides answered that transitively before; the row makes it explicit. wi1076's census grew by that row.\n\nREMAINING SCOPE: eq.anthill's `Eq provides PartialEq` half (not started — same shape, and the ONE DEPARTURE from 058 §3.8's selectability conjunct recorded earlier applies to it); kernel-language.md's Eq/Ord paragraph; library proposal 007. /code-review NOT yet run.", at: "2026-08-15T18:40:45Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "/code-review APPLIED IN PART 2026-08-15. Full workspace green after (35 binaries, 0 failed).\n\nFIXED: (15) the max/min tie-break note contradicted itself — said the tie goes to 'b' then explained 'a' wins; 'a' is right. (8) sortedset.anthill's canonicity claim, now scoped to a kernel that IS Eq, with the quotient + left-biased union stated as contract and the insertSorted arm's 'a SET, so no duplicate' comment corrected. (3, partly) ByLength reclassified to provides WeakOrd — it is the coarse witness the floor exists for, and declaring Ord meant the tests were green through a row the derivation synthesized from a FALSE claim; wi844's ordering slots and brackets swept with it. (2) identity_forward_targets now applies the conditional-provision test to the FORWARDER edge, which it was missing while the carrier edge had it — same reason, the ':- goals' tail rides in separate facts so is_fact does not see it. (4) type_param_local_name now applies is_sort_param_symbol, which it had dropped from the arms it copied — a concrete sort whose local name matched a spec param would have read as an identity forwarding. (11) the bounded fixpoint no longer exits silently. (10) wi1109_ordering_tower_test.rs, 8 tests DRIVING the acceptance with values asserted and controls beside each.\n\nDISPUTED, one finding: the review asked for ByFst/BySnd (wi858) to move to WeakOrd too. They should NOT — both are lexicographic over BOTH components, so with Ord element slots their kernel IS Eq and Ord is the honest floor. Only ByLength is coarse. LexFst (wi870) DID move, because its element slots read WeakOrd.compare and it claims a Duo order from them.\n\nMEASUREMENT THAT CORRECTED ITSELF: the new file's back-out note first claimed ONE test fails without derive_forwarded_provisions. Measured: SEVEN of eight do — Int64/String/BigInt write only 'provides Ord', so without the derived rows the stdlib's own carriers have no WeakOrd floor at all. The eighth passed VACUOUSLY (it expects load errors and got unrelated ones), so its assertion now pins the refusal's own words instead of asking that something failed.\n\nNOT FIXED, and each is a real finding worth its own pass: (1) forwarded_to_requires matches the required spec's SORT only and ignores the requires-entry BINDINGS — the binding-precise hole the sibling self_provides_required arm is explicitly written to avoid; (5) provides_spec_directly is binding-blind, so a row for the target spec at OTHER bindings suppresses the derivation actually needed; (6) assert_forwarded_provides copies every source binding rather than only those the forwarding maps, so an unmapped forwarder param lands on a target spec that has no such parameter; (7) derived rows carry no is_unbacked_derived_provision-style mark though they are asserted above check_provider_operations — WI-1103's recorded hazard; (12) the pass re-scans the whole provides relation per row per round with the index dropped, and wants one hoisted memo; (13) the compare_eq biconditional is gone even at the Ord floor where it is SOUND, so the equational/simp/proof layers lost a rewrite nothing records the loss of. Plus the standing scope: eq.anthill's Eq provides PartialEq, kernel-language.md (which still says compare lives on Ord and never mentions WeakOrd), and library proposal 007.", at: "2026-08-15T19:06:50Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "REVIEW FINDINGS 1, 5, 6 FIXED 2026-08-15 — the three binding-precision holes in new code. Full workspace green (35 binaries, 0 failed); wi1109_ordering_tower_test now 9 tests.\n\n(1) forwarded_to_requires matched the required spec's SORT and ignored the requires-entry BINDINGS, so any `requires` of that spec at any bindings discharged the forwarding. Now binding-precise — AND DRIVEN, with a measured control: `a_forwarding_is_not_discharged_by_a_requires_at_other_bindings` builds the two-parameter shape (`S requires Eq[A]`, `provides Sp[X = A, Y = B]`, `Sp requires Eq[Y]`), and backing the clause out makes it load clean — verified by actually removing the clause and re-running, not predicted.\n\n(5) provides_spec_directly asked only whether the carrier appeared among a spec's providers, so a row at OTHER bindings suppressed the derivation of the one needed. Now compares bindings.\n\n(6) assert_forwarded_provides copied every SOURCE binding; it now takes its keys from `type_params_of_sort(target)` — the eq_derive discipline — so a forwarder parameter the forwarding does not map is dropped rather than landing on a target spec that never declared it.\n\nONE THING THE FIRST DRAFT OF THESE GOT WRONG, and the suite caught it: the shared comparison was written as EQUALITY of binding lists, which refused every real entry. Measured — a `requires` entry's spec view carries the spec's OPERATION bindings beside its type parameters (`Ord requires PartialOrd[T]` decodes as [T, gt, gte, lt, lte]) while a goal carries only what it asks about ([T]). The predicate is COVERAGE, not equality: every binding the asker names must be present and agree, which keeps the property that matters (an entry bound to a different parameter cannot answer) without demanding lists that were never the same shape.\n\nHONEST NOTE ON (5) AND (6): unlike (1) they have no driving test. Their scenarios need a witness providing one spec at two different carrier bindings, and a forwarder with an unmapped parameter — neither is constructible in the corpus's current vocabulary, so both change no verdict today. They are hardening, and are recorded as such at their sites rather than credited with a fix.", at: "2026-08-15T19:31:11Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "FINDINGS 13 AND 7 CLOSED 2026-08-15. Full workspace green (35 binaries, 0 failed); wi1109_ordering_tower_test now 10 tests.\n\n(13) THE SOUND REWRITE IS BACK AT ITS FLOOR. WI-1109's first pass moved the unsound `<=>` off WeakOrd and then deleted the sound one too, so Int64/String/BigInt silently lost `compare_eq` and nothing recorded it. Restored on Ord as `rule compare_eq: eq(?a, ?b) <=> eq(WeakOrd.compare(?a, ?b), 0)`, replacing the constraint that duplicated one half of it. The split is now stated as what it is: an `<=>` is what the equational/[simp]/proof layers REWRITE with, and rewriting eq(a,b) to eq(compare(a,b),0) is valid exactly where the kernel IS Eq — at Ord, not one floor down — so WeakOrd keeps the one-directional constraint and mints no rewrite, and Ord carries the biconditional.\n\n(7) THE REVIEW'S SCENARIO DID NOT REPRODUCE; A NEIGHBOURING ONE DID, and the difference decides the remedy. Probed: `sort MyOrd { provides Ord[T = Int64] }` with no compare LOADS CLEAN — check_provider_operations skips constructor-less carriers ('abstract carrier -> sub-interface, ops may stay primitives'), so the review's own failure scenario never reaches the walk. With a CONCRETE carrier it does: `enum MyBox { entity box(v: Int64)  provides Ord[T = MyBox] }` was refused for \"provides 'WeakOrd' but backs no operation 'WeakOrd.compare'\" — word for word what a carrier that HAD written `provides WeakOrd` gets (measured side by side).\n\nSO THE MARK WOULD HAVE BEEN THE WRONG FIX. eq_derive's is_unbacked_derived_provision exists because a derived NonEq's witness is a PROPAGATED CLASSIFICATION, not an operation the carrier owes. A forwarded WeakOrd row's `compare` IS owed — a concrete Ord carrier without one was already refused before the split, when Ord declared compare itself — so exempting it would suppress a CORRECT refusal. The defect is attribution, not the refusal.\n\nWHAT LANDED: a second KB map, `derived_provision_origin` (RuleId -> source spec), recorded at the derivation's mint site and read by the diagnostic. PROVENANCE, NOT EXEMPTION, and it is a separate map from unbacked_derived_provisions precisely so the two cannot be confused. The refusal now appends \"note 'WeakOrd' is not written on this carrier: it is DERIVED from its `provides Ord`, because 'Ord' forwards to 'WeakOrd'. Back the operation, or drop the `provides Ord`\". Both message faces (Display and the located one) go through ONE owner, the bare_member_call_message discipline, so the clause cannot reach one wording and miss the other.\n\nDRIVEN with its control: `a_derived_provision_names_the_clause_the_author_wrote` asserts the derived carrier gets the note AND that the author-written one does NOT — without the second half the note could be unconditional and the first would not discriminate. The test records why both carriers declare an entity: a constructor-less sort never reaches this walk.", at: "2026-08-15T20:30:56Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "FINDING 12 FIXED 2026-08-15, and it was not a micro-optimization. Full workspace green (35 binaries, 0 failed).\n\nMEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER (ANTHILL_LOAD_TIMING=1 over the stdlib), because the review stated the cost as a complexity argument and a complexity argument is not a number: the per-row shape cost 266.8 ms and the hoisted one costs 4.4 ms — 61x, against a whole-load budget of roughly 135 ms. So the version that shipped through the last four rounds of this ticket VERY NEARLY TRIPLED THE TIME TO LOAD THE STANDARD LIBRARY, on every load, and nothing in the suite would ever have said so. The control was the same loop with the indexes rebuilt inside it; if anything it UNDERSTATES the original, which also re-scanned for the already-provided check.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is the placement's fault rather than the code's: the pass deliberately runs with kb.provides_index = None (it asserts into the relation it reads), so every helper it called degraded from an indexed lookup to a FULL SCAN of the provision relation — and it called three of them per row per round. That is O(rounds x rows x relation). Nothing about the calls looked expensive at the call site; the index being absent is what made them so, which is exactly the kind of cost a reviewer reads off the structure and an author does not feel.\n\nWHAT LANDED: one decode pass per round (decoded_provision_rows) feeding three hoisted indexes — the identity-forwarding map (forwarder -> floors), the (carrier, spec) -> existing-bindings map for the binding-precise suppression, and one sweep of the condition facts (conditioned_provision_pairs). The two scanning helpers are gone; what remains of them is is_identity_forwarding, a pure predicate with no scan, so the identity criterion still has ONE owner. The number is recorded at decoded_provision_rows' doc rather than in this ticket alone, so the next person to touch the loop reads why it is shaped this way.\n\nREVIEW STATUS: 13 of 15 findings closed (1, 2, 3-partly, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12, 13, 15). One DISPUTED and not applied (ByFst/BySnd are lexicographic over both components, so their kernel IS Eq and Ord is the honest floor). One is the standing doc scope (9): kernel-language.md still says compare lives on Ord and never mentions WeakOrd, and library proposal 007 is unwritten — with eq.anthill's Eq provides PartialEq half alongside them.", at: "2026-08-15T20:48:16Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1109", author: "user", content: "DOC SCOPE DONE, AND THE CODE HALF ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT 2026-08-15. Full workspace green (35 binaries, 0 failed).\n\nkernel-language.md: the ordering block and the `=` section's ordering parenthetical now state three floors, the C++20 correspondence, and SortedSet's quotient consequence. The illustrative snippet says why the shipped WeakOrd declares only `compare` (declaring gt/lt on two specs is the HashMap-iteration-order coin flip ordered.anthill warns about).\n\nLibrary proposal 007 written and indexed, continuing 004 as 004 continues 051.\n\n`Eq provides PartialEq` — NOT DONE, and the reason recorded is not the one I first gave. Both variants driven: requires+provides = 1867 of 2849 fail (cyclic); provides alone = 32 fail, every one `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Eq.eq'`. THE SECOND IS THE REASON — `requires` is what makes `Eq.eq` A NAME (004 kept it resolving through the requires-chain for source compatibility; `provides` puts nothing in a spec's own scope), so the two clauses are mutually exclusive and the one keeping written names working wins.\n\nAND THE CYCLE IS NOT PECULIAR TO EQUALITY — I claimed it was, twice, and both claims are now corrected at their sites. `Ord` carries both clauses and cycles identically; it is MASKED because `WeakOrd requires PartialOrd` fails first for most carriers. My earlier probe used a carrier with NO provisions, failed at PartialOrd, and I read that as absence rather than as an earlier failure hiding it. Driven with a carrier that has Eq and PartialOrd but no compare: `construction is cyclic: WeakOrd[T = Half] -> WeakOrd[T = Half]`. Filed as WI-1110 with the Stream/Iterable risk that keeps it from being a one-line fix.", at: "2026-08-15T21:27:04Z")
